{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 220,
    "dDurationMs": 3990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With all the talk these days about \"leaping\nyears,\" we should probably be asking \"what"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4210,
    "dDurationMs": 1049,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is a year, anyway?\""
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5259,
    "dDurationMs": 4651,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ignoring all cultural history, a year is basically\nthe period of time that it takes for one physical"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9910,
    "dDurationMs": 1570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "body to orbit another."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11480,
    "dDurationMs": 3110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So an earth month could equally be called\na \"moon year.\""
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14590,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But to under-simplify things, even if we stay\non earth, there are several different ways"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 18310,
    "dDurationMs": 2629,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to define a year\n— either the time it takes for the tilt"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20939,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the earth's axis to come back to the same\nangle relative to the sun (called a tropical"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25079,
    "dDurationMs": 2341,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "year)\n— or the amount of time before we come back"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27420,
    "dDurationMs": 3829,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and see the same stars rising behind the sun\n(called a sidereal year - pronounced like"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 31249,
    "dDurationMs": 1651,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ethereal)\n— or, since the earth follows an elliptical"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 32900,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "orbit which precesses, we could measure the\namount of time between closest approaches"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36340,
    "dDurationMs": 2610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the sun (this is called the anomalistic\nyear)."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38950,
    "dDurationMs": 3010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Of course, all three \"years\" have slightly\ndifferent lengths."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 41960,
    "dDurationMs": 3650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But our day-to-day civil calendar does its\nbest to follow the tropical year, since its"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 45610,
    "dDurationMs": 4100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "duration is defined by the tilt of the earth's\naxis which also determines the passage of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 49710,
    "dDurationMs": 1250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the seasons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50960,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are non-earth years, too: Mercury's\nyear is roughly 88 earth days, Neptune's is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56060,
    "dDurationMs": 4810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "165 earth years, and the \"Galactic\" year – which\nis how long the whole solar system takes to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60870,
    "dDurationMs": 4070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "orbit once around the Milky Way – is around\na quarter of a billion earth years!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 64940,
    "dDurationMs": 4670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But not all galactic years are long: check\nout this timelapse of stars orbiting the supermassive"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 69610,
    "dDurationMs": 2300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole at the center of the Milky Way!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71910,
    "dDurationMs": 3010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Even though you can't see the black hole,\nit's pretty obvious that it's there, what"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 74920,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with all those stars zipping around in orbits\ntaking only ten earth years!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78580,
    "dDurationMs": 3530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Astrophysicists have even used these orbits\nto figure out that the black hole is as massive"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82110,
    "dDurationMs": 1740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as 4 million suns."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 83850,
    "dDurationMs": 3230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But enough about interstellar timekeeping…\nif you want to learn more about leap years"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 87080,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "here on Earth, check out Grey's new video\nover on his awesome channel, \"cgpgrey\""
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 93600,
    "dDurationMs": 4150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And a big thanks to the UCLA Galactic Center\nfor doing sweet supermassive black hole research"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 97750,
    "dDurationMs": 3250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with the Keck telescope in Hawai'i, and for\nsharing their images with the public."
    } ]
  } ]
}
